<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vosges</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Vosges Mountains</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vosges are a range of low mountains in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, running parallel to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> on the western side of the Upper Rhine plain and separating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>. Their highest summit, the Grand Ballon, reaches 1,424 m. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the range lay within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, with abbeys, small principalities and imperial towns on its slopes; a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> also bears its name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vosges.jpg" alt="Vosges" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tangopaso · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Houses_near_Bussang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
